> Low-bit selection, zero width and checked inverse boundaries

The original API explicitly converts the low num_bits bits. It keeps
that behavior: requesting four bits of 243 selects binary 0011, value
three; requesting eight retains 243. Zero bits select no input and
return the singleton zero digit, with no stages. Width 64 retains the
entire uint64_t input. Higher input bits are deliberately ignored, rather
than treated as an input-range error.

Widths outside 0…64 now throw invalid_argument before allocation or
shifts. The unchanged old header accepts width −1 as zero, and a
compiled sanitizer probe diagnoses its shift by 64 for requested width
65. The canonical kernel branches explicitly for zero/full width,
uses no shift by 64 and obtains capacity from exact integer lookup.

The inverse bcd_to_uint64 accepts nonempty unpacked digits 0…9 and
leading zeros. It validates the complete digit domain before checked
multiply-ten/add evaluation, rejecting empty vectors, invalid digits
and values larger than UINT64_MAX. It is an ordinary checked decoder,
not an implementation of reverse shift/subtract-three. Ordinary decimal
string formatting and that inverse are separate operations from the
shift/add-three conversion recurrence.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dabble_widths.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::print("| input | bits | retained value | decimal digits | text |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for(const auto [value,bits]:std::vector<std::pair<std::uint64_t,int>>{{243,0},{243,1},{243,4},{243,8},{std::numeric_limits<std::uint64_t>::max(),63},{std::numeric_limits<std::uint64_t>::max(),64}}) {
    const auto t=dm::algorithms::double_dabble_trace(value,bits);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",value,bits,t.retained_value,t.digits,dm::algorithms::double_dabble_string(value,bits));
  }
  int rejected_width=0,rejected_bcd=0,overflow=0;
  for(int b:{std::numeric_limits<int>::min(),-1,65,std::numeric_limits<int>::max()}) {
    try {(void)dm::algorithms::double_dabble(0,b);} catch(const std::invalid_argument&) {++rejected_width;}
  }
  for(const auto digits:std::vector<std::vector<int>>{{},{1,10},{-1}}) {
    try {(void)dm::algorithms::bcd_to_uint64(digits);} catch(const std::invalid_argument&) {++rejected_bcd;}
  }
  std::vector<int> too_large;
  for(char c:std::string("18446744073709551616")) too_large.push_back(c-'0');
  try {(void)dm::algorithms::bcd_to_uint64(too_large);} catch(const std::overflow_error&) {++overflow;}
  std::print("\nExpected invalid widths {}/4, invalid BCD vectors {}/3, overflows {}/1. Leading-zero decode {}.\n",rejected_width,rejected_bcd,overflow,dm::algorithms::bcd_to_uint64({0,0,2,4,3}));
  const auto narrow=dm::algorithms::double_dabble_trace(243,4);
  const auto full=dm::algorithms::double_dabble_trace(243,8);
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(78,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=4;box.height=1.2;box.font_size=24;
  d.node("input",{0,2},"input 243\nbinary 11110011",box)
   .node("narrow",{-3.2,-.4},std::format("width 4: low 0011\nretained {} → digits {}",narrow.retained_value,dm::algorithms::double_dabble_string(243,4)),box)
   .node("full",{3.2,-.4},std::format("width 8: all 11110011\nretained {} → digits {}",full.retained_value,dm::algorithms::double_dabble_string(243,8)),box)
   .edge("input","narrow","select 4 bits").edge("input","full","select 8 bits")
   .text({0,3.35},"Requested width determines which binary value is converted",25)
   .save_svg("build/dabble_widths.svg");
}
